          %title Universes in SEAR
          %h1#universes_in_sear Universes in SEAR
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#using_families"} Using families
              %li
                %a{:href => "#using_mere_sets"} Using mere sets
          %p
            This page develops a theory of universes in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            and the theory of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+a+topos"} universe in a topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+in+SEAR"} categories in SEAR
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: Can we talk about Grothendieck universes or analogous size-related mechanisms? (this is a naive question, since there may be (no need for/obvious way to do) this in a structural set theory that I am unaware of.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: Universes can be defined in any structural set theory in a fairly straightforward way, modulo the usual translation from “set of sets” to “family of sets.” There is some stuff from a more ETCS-like perspective at
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+a+topos"} universe in a topos
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: I’d certainly like to, subject to real-world time constraints. It seems to me that something could be done to the axiom of collection if we had a axiom of universes (for every set there is a universe acting as a universal family for it, or similar).
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: I’m not sure that Collection can really be eliminated using universes, but maybe it can.
          %h2#using_families Using families
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: Some sketchy thoughts - a universe will be a relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \looparrowright E
              satisfying axioms analogous to those at
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+a+topos"} universe in a topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \looparrowright A
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  Pullback - unless I am mistaken, the appropriate version is this: Given a function
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                          %mo →
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to U
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                        %mo ↬
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \looparrowright E
                  should be regarded as the 'pullback' family. This family should be isomorphic to the family
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                          %mo ↬
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \looparrowright A
              %li
                %p Dependent product - this is tricker and I’m not going to do that right now.
          %p
            Fix a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \looparrowright E
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A family
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:I \looparrowright A
              is called
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                \-small
              if there is a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:I \to U
              such that for the composite relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \looparrowright E
              …
          %p
            A set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is called
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}\looparrowright A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            for all elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p For ease of language, we define a composite of families to be the composite relation of the two relations defining the families.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition:
            %p
              A family of sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \looparrowright E
              is called a
              %em universe
              if the following axioms are satisfied:
            %ul
              %li
                The composite of two
                = succeed "-small" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "-small" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %li
                The relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mi o
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ↬
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^o:B\looparrowright A
                opposite to an injective function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \to B
                is a
                = succeed "-small" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
